    {
      "type": "done",
      "answer": "No, the lecture does not state that *all* GPU streaming multiprocessors (SMs) support at most 1,536 resident threads. It specifically cites this limit for the **Fermi** architecture.\n\nAccording to the lecture, a thread block is assigned to one SM, and up to 8 blocks can be assigned to each SM. The text explicitly states that a \"Fermi SM can take up to 1536 threads\" [S2]. It provides examples of how this capacity can be configured, such as 256 threads per block times 6 blocks, or 512 threads per block times 3 blocks [S2].\n\nThe lecture describes the general organization of an SM, noting that it contains processing units, a warp scheduler, shared memory, and tensor cores [S3]. However, it does not provide a universal thread limit for all GPU architectures, only the specific example of the Fermi SM. Therefore, based on the provided material, we cannot conclude that 1,536 is the maximum for all SMs, only for the Fermi generation mentioned.\n\nSee page 37 [S2].",
      "citations": [
        {
          "document_id": "34911c46e78edc833f283f8dd678b85d6f8c92d66859e6bf6886949181d95402",
          "chunk_id": "34911c46e78edc833f283f8dd678b85d6f8c92d66859e6bf6886949181d95402-p37-1",
          "page": 37,
          "text": "GPU: Optimize Throughput of a Large Number of Simple Threads\n  Example: Executing Thread Blocks\n\n¡ A thread block is assigned to one SM\n\n   ¡ Up to 8 blocks to each SM\n\n   ¡  Fermi SM can take up to 1536 threads\n     ¡ Could be 256 * 6 blocks\n\n     ¡  Or 512 * 3 blocks, etc.\n\n\n\n   ¡  Each block is divided into 32-thread warps\n\n      ¡  Scheduling by the warp scheduler within a SM.\n\n   ¡  Single Instruction, Multiple Threads (SIMT)\n       abstraction\n\n      ¡  Threads in a wrap should execution same\n           codes/instructions.\n\n      ¡  However, thread is allowed to execute different\n          branches.",
          "preview_url": "/api/documents/34911c46e78edc833f283f8dd678b85d6f8c92d66859e6bf6886949181d95402/pages/37/preview",
          "source": "S2"
        },
